To apply a Gaussian filter before Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, follow these steps:

1. Define the size of the Gaussian filter kernel (e.g., 3x3 or 5x5).

2. Compute the Gaussian filter by generating a 2D Gaussian function with zero mean and standard deviation as the chosen value for smoothing. Then, convolve the filtered image with the Gaussian kernel using `conv2()` function.

3. Apply the modified Sobel edge detection algorithm described below.

Here's the modified MATLAB code:

```matlab
% Load image
img = imread('image.jpg');
% Convert RGB image to grayscale
grayImg = rgb2gray(img);

% Define size of Gaussian filter kernel
kernelSize = 5;

% Generate Gaussian filter kernel
[gaussianFilterKernel, gaussianFilterKernelFreq] = fspecial('gaussian', [kernelSize kernelSize], 0.5);

% Apply Gaussian filter
filteredImg = filter2(gaussianFilterKernel, grayImg, 'replicate');

% Compute gradients using Sobel operator
sobelX = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobelY = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
gradientX = conv2(filteredImg, sobelX, 'same');
gradientY = conv2(filteredImg, sobelY, 'same');

% Calculate magnitude and orientation of gradient
magnitude = sqrt(gradientX.^2 + gradientY.^2);
orientation = atan2(gradientY, gradientX) * 180 / pi;

% Display original and processed images
figure;
subplot(2,2,1);
imshow(grayImg);
title('Original Image');
subplot(2,2,2);
imshow(filteredImg);
title('Gaussian Filtered Image');
subplot(2,2,3);
imshow(magnitude);
title('Edge Magnitude Image');
subplot(2,2,4);
imshow(orientation);

