 Here's how you can modify the CSS properties of the `.circle` class to make it look more 3D:

1. Add `perspective` property to the parent element (e.g. `#chart-area`) with a value of at least 800px. This will create a 3D space for our circle to be in.

```css
#chart-area {
  perspective: 800px;
}
```

2. Add `transform-style` property to the `.circle` class with a value of `preserve-3d`. This will enable us to rotate and move the circle around in 3D space.

```css
.circle {
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
}
```

3. Add `transform` property to the `.circle` class to rotate the circle along the Y-axis by -10 degrees. This will give it a tilt effect.

```css
.circle {
  transform: rotateY(-10deg) translateZ(50px);
}
```

4. Increase the `border-radius` of the `.circle` class to make it more rounded. You may need to adjust this value based on your preference.

```css
.circle {
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

5. Adjust the `box-shadow` property to add a shadow to the circle. The first two values represent horizontal and vertical offset, the third and fourth values represent blur radius, and the fifth value represents spread radius.

```css
.circle {
  box-shadow: 0px 0px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3), 0px 0px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
}
```

With these changes, your circle should now have a more 3D look and feel! You can further tweak the CSS properties to achieve the desired effect.

