To implement this logic in JavaScript, you can create a function that takes the input array and returns a new array with items shuffled based on their position and randomization preference. Here's the code:

```js
function shuffleArray(arr) {
  let result = [];
  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const { name, position, randomize } = arr[i];
    if (!randomize) {
      result[position - 1] = name;
    } else {
      result.push(name);
    }
  }
  return result;
}

const inputArr = [
  { name: "apple", position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: "pineapple", position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: "orange", position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: "banana", position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: "grapes", position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: "lemon", position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: "jack fruit", position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: "dragon fruit", position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: "green apple", position: 9, randomize: false },
];

const outputArr = shuffleArray(inputArr);
console.log(outputArr);
```

In this implementation, we loop through the input array and add each item to the result array based on its `randomize` property. If `randomize` is false, we insert the item at its specified position using array indexing. Otherwise, we simply push it onto the end of the array. This ensures that items with `randomize` set to false appear in their correct positions, while items with `randomize` set to true are randomly shuffled.

