Here's the implementation of `RedisClient` in `redis.js`:

```javascript
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on('error', (err) => {
      console.error(`Redis client not connected: ${err.message}`);
    });
  }

  async isAlive() {
    try {
      await this.client.ping();
      return true;
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Error checking Redis connectivity: ${err.message}`);
      return false;
    }
  }

  async get(key) {
    return this.client.get(key);
  }

  async set(key, value, duration) {
    return this.client.setex(key, duration, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    return this.client.del(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

In this implementation, we first import the `redis` module from Node.js and then define our `RedisClient` class. The constructor initializes a new `redis` client with the default configuration and adds an event listener for the `error` event. We log any errors that occur during connection attempts to the console.

The `isAlive` method checks whether the Redis connection is still active by sending a `PING` command to the server. If the server responds, the method returns `true`. Otherwise, it logs an error message and returns `false`.

